Subject: Analysis of Proposed 5% Rate Increase in Regard to Headline CPI Increases
Dunning and Letter Mailings has proposed a 5% increase in service rates under Annex B of the Pricing and Payment section in its agreement with Aquarion Water Company. Under this provision, rate adjustments are to be made in proportion to changes in the Consumer Price Index (CPI-U) and further modified by the Market Price Multiplier (MPX). It may be concluded that their costs are tied to inputs such as paper, envelopes, postage, and labor, all of which have faced varying levels of pressures in price in recent months. 
The company is most likely seeking a simple, uniform adjustment that anticipates future inflation or overstates current cost pressures, rather than one strictly tied to the actual benchmarks outlined in the agreement. It is also worth noting that a future Federal Reserve rate cut could add upward pressure to inflation by stimulating consumer demand and raising import costs, making CPI more likely to climb in this regard.
As you may know, the most recent data from the Bureau of Labor Statistics shows that the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers (CPI-U) rose 2.7 percent over the past 12 months, with a seasonally adjusted increase of 0.2 percent in July 2025. For these reasons, I would advise that a 5% adjustment is unjustified and out of the question under the current agreement. Additionally, the costs underlying their services, such as paper, envelopes, and printing, are more closely tied to PPI (Producer Price Index) measures, which have also not increased by 5% over the past year. 
Lastly, it’s important to acknowledge the Market Price Multiplier (MPX). Unless MPX is explicitly defined in the Pricing and Payment section, it carries no contractual weight and cannot justify an increase beyond the actual CPI-U figure of 2.7%. If the MPX is defined as >1 in the contract, as theoretically it would determine why CPI adjustments can be scaled beyond the reported 2.7%. However, in my respectful opinion, even once the MPX value is identified, a 5% increase still remains unjustifiable. MPX is not an established economic measure but rather a company-defined figure, and therefore it cannot serve as a valid basis for exceeding the actual CPI-U adjustment of 2.7%.
I do find it somewhat ironic that the company ties the value of all services to the Consumer Price Index (CPI-U), when a more accurate measure would be the Producer Price Index (PPI), which directly reflects the input costs of paper, envelopes, ink, and mailing services in the B2B Industry. Using CPI instead of PPI may be a deliberate choice to avoid the volatility often found in producer prices, which can swing more sharply month to month. But even with that consideration, anchoring rate adjustments to consumer inflation rather than producer costs creates a disconnect between the index being used and the actual drivers of expenses in this industry. In effect, the clause measures inflation from the perspective of households rather than the real supply-side costs of running a paper and mailing services operation.
Thank you.
